<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg-residence"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wurzburg-residence.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Würzburg Residence</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Würzburg Residence is the baroque palace of the prince-bishops of Würzburg in Bavaria, Germany, built mainly between 1720 and 1744 to designs by Balthasar Neumann.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wurzen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wurzen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Wurzen</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Wurzen is a town on the Mulde east of Leipzig in Saxony, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wushe-incident"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wushe-incident.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Wushe Incident</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt · 1930</span><span class="enc-snip">The Wushe Incident (Musha Incident) was an uprising of Seediq people against Japanese colonial rule at Wushe (Musha) in central Taiwan in October 1930.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wusun"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wusun.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Wusun</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Wusun were a nomadic people of Central Asia known from Chinese sources.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wuwei"><span class="enc-name">Wuwei</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Wuwei is a city in Gansu province in north-western China, on the eastern part of the Hexi Corridor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wuyuan-commandery"><span class="enc-name">Wuyuan Commandery</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Wuyuan Commandery was a frontier commandery of Han China, created in 127 BCE on the great northern bend of the Yellow River, around modern Baotou in Inner Mongolia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wuzhile"><span class="enc-name">Wuzhile</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Wuzhile was the founder of Turgesh power in Central Asia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wuzurg-framadar"><span class="enc-name">Wuzurg framadar</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The wuzurg framadar was the chief minister of the Sasanian Empire, often described as its grand vizier.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wuzurgan"><span class="enc-name">Wuzurgan</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The wuzurgan were the great noble houses of the Sasanian Empire, whose military power and regional authority the dynasty could not entirely suppress.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wyandot"><span class="enc-name">Wyandot</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Wyandot are an Indigenous people formed in the 17th century from Wendat and Petun refugees after the Iroquois destroyed their homelands in 1649-1650.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wybalenna"><span class="enc-name">Wybalenna</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Wybalenna was the settlement on Flinders Island, Tasmania, where the surviving Aboriginal Tasmanians were confined from 1833, later under George Augustus Robinson.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wyke-cruz-treaty"><span class="enc-name">Wyke–Cruz Treaty</span><span class="enc-type">Treaty · 1859</span><span class="enc-snip">The Wyke–Cruz Treaty was signed in November 1859 by Britain, represented by Charles Lennox Wyke, and Honduras, represented by Francisco Cruz.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/waerferth-of-worcester"><span class="enc-name">Wærferth of Worcester</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Wærferth was Bishop of Worcester from about 872 to 915, one of four Mercian scholars whom Alfred the Great brought to his court to revive learning.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Władysław I Łokietek</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław I Łokietek, 'the Elbow-high', was a Piast duke of Kuyavia who spent decades fighting rival dukes, Bohemia and the Teutonic Knights for the fragmented Polish…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Władysław II Jagiełło</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław II Jagiełło, known in Lithuanian as Jogaila, was Grand Duke of Lithuania who became King of Poland after marrying Queen Jadwiga under the Union of Krewo of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Władysław II of Opole</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław II of Opole, known as Opolczyk, was a Piast Duke of Opole in Silesia who served the Angevin kings as palatine of Hungary and governor of Galicia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-plock"><span class="enc-name">Władysław II of Płock</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław II of Płock was a Piast duke of Masovia who ruled the lands of Płock, Rawa, Gostynin and neighbouring districts jointly with his elder brother Siemowit VI…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-the-exile"><span class="enc-name">Władysław II the Exile</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław II the Exile was the eldest son of Bolesław III Wrymouth and, under his father's testament of 1138, High Duke of Poland and Duke of Silesia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-iii-of-poland"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-iii-of-poland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Władysław III of Poland</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław III was king of Poland from 1434 and, as Vladislaus I, king of Hungary from 1440 until his death.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-iv-vasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-iv-vasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Władysław IV Vasa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław IV Vasa was King of Poland and Grand Duke of Lithuania from 1632 to 1648.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-odonic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-odonic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Władysław Odonic</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław Odonic was a Piast Duke of Greater Poland who fought his uncle Władysław Spindleshanks for the duchy and won it after 1229.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-of-bytom"><span class="enc-name">Władysław of Bytom</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław of Bytom was a Silesian Piast duke who ruled Koźle and Bytom in the first half of the 14th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-of-dobrzyn"><span class="enc-name">Władysław of Dobrzyń</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław of Dobrzyń, called the Humpback, was a Kuyavian Piast duke and nephew of King Władysław the Elbow-high.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-of-opole"><span class="enc-name">Władysław of Opole</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław of Opole was a Piast Duke of Opole and Racibórz in Upper Silesia from 1246 to 1281.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-sicinski"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-sicinski.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Władysław Siciński</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław Siciński was a deputy to the Sejm of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-the-white"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-the-white.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Władysław the White</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Władysław the White was the last Duke of Gniewkowo, of the Kuyavian branch of the Piast dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/wloclawek"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/wloclawek.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Włocławek</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Włocławek is a city in central Poland on the Vistula, in the historical region of Kuyavia.</span></a></li></ul>
